  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 536 AM CDT Mon Aug 3 2026\n\nAn occluded upper low is spinning from Saskatchewan into \nManitoba early this morning. A positively tilted trough axis \nextends southwestward from the low, with a shortwave at its base\npreparing to eject off the Northern Rockies. An inverted \nsurface trough is analyzed from a low pressure over the Black \nHills to the northern Red River Valley. The inverted trough \nextends vertically, with strong 850-700 mb warm air advection, \nfrontogenesis, and moisture transport. This has resulted in \nscattered showers and isolated thunderstorms developing across \nmuch of western and central North Dakota early this morning. The\nmost recent suite of CAMs was far too slow in developing this \nactivity, but they do consolidate on a broader area of more \npersistent showers and thunderstorms shifting from south central\ninto eastern North Dakota around 6 to 9 AM CDT. The convection \nis presently and forecast to remain elevated, with effective \nbulk shear ranging from as high as 55 kts from southwest to \ncentral North Dakota to as low as 30 kts in the southeast. So \nfar, it appears that updrafts have not been able to realize the \nfull extent of this shear and 1000-2000 J/kg MUCAPE, but recent \nmodel trends suggest this may change closer to sunrise. Periods \nof scattered showers and isolated thunderstorms are likely to \ncontinue through the afternoon as the upstream shortwave moves \ninto the Northern Plains, but 00-06Z initialized CAMs have \nalmost completely shifted any severe threat east of our forecast\narea. The HREF and RAP do maintain 1000-2000 J/kg of MUCAPE in \nthe James River Valley through the afternoon though, with \neffective bulk shear there increasing closer to 50 kts by late \nafternoon. Any afternoon convection in this part of the state \nshould also remain elevated, which keeps our confidence in the \nthreat ceiling low.\n\nThe other concern for today is northerly winds increasing to \naround 25 to 30 mph with gusts as high as 40 mph through this \nafternoon. The upstream shortwave will bring a stronger push of \ncooler and drier air into the state, limiting highs to the upper\n60s in the far northwest but still reaching the 80s across the \nsouth. Humidity falling as low as 25 percent in southwest North \nDakota keeps critical fire weather conditions in the forecast \nthrough early evening. A Red Flag Warning remains in effect for \nmost areas south and west of the Missouri River. See the fire \nsection below for details.\n\nSurface high pressure will begin building into the region on \nTuesday as longwave troughing resides overhead. Forecast highs \nremain in the 70s for all of western and central North Dakota on\nTuesday, and both tonight and Tuesday night could see \nwidespread lows in the mid to upper 40s. Through the rest of the\nweek, the longwave trough is forecast to slowly shift eastward,\nallowing the western CONUS upper ridge to build back closer to \nthe Northern High Plains. This brings a projected warming trend \nheading into the weekend, with the NBM advertising highs in the \n90s returning to the southwest quadrant of the state Friday and\nSaturday. The median 50th percentile spreads do increase to \n10-15 degrees Fahrenheit, but the cooler side of the \ndistribution shows a clear warming trend through at least \nFriday. Ensembles generally favor a transition to a quasi-zonal \nflow pattern this weekend into early next week, but there is at \nleast one cluster each day that maintains northwest flow with an\nupper level ridge axis between the Northern Rockies and \nNorthern Plains. After today, chances for measurable rain \nremain very low through the end of the week.",
